# Changelog - xcesp-l2tpv3d

## 0.3.0

- **Tunnel state machine** (`xcesp_l2tpv3d/tunnel_fsm.py`) — the RFC
  3931 §7.2.1 state matrix: `IDLE` → `WAIT_CTL_REPLY` (initiator sent
  SCCRQ) or `WAIT_CTL_CONN` (responder sent SCCRP) → `ESTABLISHED` →
  `SEND_STOPCCN` for teardown.  Handles both symmetric roles (either
  peer can initiate) plus peer-restart (SCCRQ received while
  ESTABLISHED → tear down and back to IDLE).  Time-agnostic like the
  transport `Peer` — callers drive it via ``on_local_open``,
  ``on_local_close``, ``on_message``, ``on_hello_timer``,
  ``on_peer_dead``; the FSM returns typed ``Action`` objects
  (``SendMessage``, ``SetHelloTimer``, ``ClearHelloTimer``,
  ``Established``, ``TornDown``) the caller executes.
- **Typed message builders** (`messages.py`) — one factory per RFC
  3931 §6.x message: ``build_sccrq_avps``, ``build_sccrp_avps``,
  ``build_scccn_avps``, ``build_hello_avps``, ``build_stopccn_avps``.
  Each populates the mandatory AVPs (Router ID, Assigned Control
  Connection ID, Pseudowire Capabilities List, Host Name, Receive
  Window Size) plus optional AVPs (Vendor Name, Firmware Revision,
  Control Message Authentication Nonce).  Symmetric parsers
  ``parse_sccrx_fields`` / ``parse_stopccn_fields`` extract typed
  ``SccrxFields`` / ``StopCcnFields`` dataclasses from received AVP
  lists, with mandatory-field enforcement.
- **Message Digest AVP authentication** (`auth.py` + `messages.py`) —
  HMAC-MD5 or HMAC-SHA1 per RFC 3931 §5.4.3 + §4.4.  The signing
  input is ``0x01 || full-message-bytes-with-digest-zeroed`` and the
  HMAC key is the pre-shared secret.  Exposed via
  ``ControlMessage.encode_signed(hash_type, secret)`` and
  ``ControlMessage.decode_and_verify(buf, hash_type, secret)``.
  Verification is position-independent (locates the Message Digest
  AVP by parsing, not by fixed offset), uses ``hmac.compare_digest``
  for timing-safety, and rejects on any of: missing digest AVP, wrong
  hash type, wrong length, or verification failure.
- **AVP additions** — Receive Window Size, Vendor Name, Firmware
  Revision, Control Message Authentication Nonce (attr 73) builders
  and decoders.  ``find_avp`` helper for message parsers to grab a
  single AVP by (vendor, type) without hand-iterating.
- **TOML config loader** (`config.py`) — `[global]` section
  (log_level / listen_address / listen_port) plus one or more
  `[[tunnel]]` blocks with strict validation and helpful error
  messages.  Router ID accepts both dotted-quad strings and 32-bit
  ints.  Passwords enable Message Digest AVP auth.  Range checks on
  hello_interval / retransmit_interval / max_retries / receive_window
  match RFC 3931 §5.8 guidance.  Uses Python 3.11+ stdlib ``tomllib``
  (no external dep).
- **Daemon entry point** (`main.py`) — asyncio event loop that binds
  a single UDP socket via ``UdpTransport``, instantiates one
  ``Tunnel`` (bundling a transport ``Peer`` + protocol-layer
  ``TunnelFSM``) per configured entry, dispatches inbound datagrams
  by Control Connection ID, drives a 0.5 s retransmit-and-HELLO
  tick, sends ``sd_notify`` READY/STOPPING to systemd, and handles
  SIGTERM / SIGINT gracefully.  Signed encoding + verification is
  invoked automatically when a tunnel has ``password`` configured.
- **Structured logging** (`log.py`) — thin ``logging`` wrapper with
  per-tunnel loggers.  stderr → journald.
- **Systemd unit** (`contrib/systemd/xcesp-l2tpv3d.service`) —
  ``Type=notify`` for readiness reporting, capabilities set to just
  what's needed (``CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`` for port 1701 +
  ``CAP_NET_ADMIN`` for the 0.4.0+ dataplane netlink), aggressive
  sandboxing (``ProtectSystem``, ``ProtectHome``,
  ``ProtectKernelTunables``, restricted address families).
- **Example config** (`contrib/cfg/xcesp-l2tpv3d.toml.example`) —
  drop-in template covering every documented knob with default
  values and Cisco-interop pointers.
- **Integration tests** (`test/test_integration.py`) — two ``Tunnel``
  objects wired via ``LoopbackTransport`` handshake to ESTABLISHED,
  exchange HELLOs for several rounds, tear down via local close, and
  the negative-path where mismatched shared secrets prevent
  establishment.  Runs entirely in-process without root or real UDP.
- **Bugfix in transport.Peer.receive** — now accepts messages with
  Control Connection ID 0 (initial SCCRQ before the peer has learned
  our Assigned CCID) OR ``local_ccid`` (post-handshake).  The old
  0.2.0 strict check rejected every SCCRQ, which only mattered once
  the FSM layer above tried to negotiate.

- **Test count 117 → 206** (+89 new).  Full suite green in ~12 s
  (0.5 s for pure unit + ~11 s for the async integration pump loops).

## 0.2.0

- **ControlMessage wire codec** (`xcesp_l2tpv3d/messages.py`) — the 12-byte
  L2TPv3 control-message header per RFC 3931 §4.1.1: T/L/S flag bits,
  4 reserved bits (validated zero on decode), 4-bit Version (=3),
  Length (16-bit), Control Connection ID (32-bit), Ns (16-bit), Nr
  (16-bit).  Encode + decode + ZLB detection.  Rejects data messages
  (T=0), missing L/S bits, non-zero reserved bits, wrong version,
  length/buffer mismatch, out-of-range CCID/Ns/Nr.
- **Sequence-number arithmetic** (`seq_delta`, `seq_advance`) — wrap-
  safe 16-bit comparison per RFC 3931 §5.8 (values in [0, 32767] mean
  "ahead", values in [-32768, -1] mean "behind"; the 0x8000 boundary
  is exactly `-0x8000` per L2TP half-window semantics).
- **Peer state machine** (`xcesp_l2tpv3d/transport.py`) — per-remote
  reliable delivery.  Time-agnostic: caller passes a monotonic `now`
  on every call, so retransmit behaviour is deterministically
  testable against a mocked clock.  Owns:
    * `_ns_send` / `_nr_expect` counters (16-bit, wrap-safe).
    * Send queue of unacked messages, cleared as peer's Nr advances.
    * Reorder buffer bounded by `receive_window`.
    * Retransmit timer with RFC 3931 §5.8 exponential back-off:
      1s → 2s → 4s → 8s → 16s (capped, `RETRANSMIT_MAX_INTERVAL`).
    * Dead-peer detection when `max_retries` exceeded.
    * ZLB (Zero-Length Body) ack-only messages that don't consume Ns
      and aren't themselves acked.
    * Nr-refresh on retransmit (so the resent copy carries our latest
      acknowledgement, not the stale Nr from first transmission).
- **Transport abstraction** (`Transport` ABC + `UdpTransport` for
  asyncio production + `LoopbackTransport` for tests).  Loopback
  variant uses a shared in-memory fabric dict; supports `drop_next`
  for injecting packet loss in tests.
- **Unit tests** — 48 new (+69 from 0.1.0 = 117 total, 0.20s green):
    * ControlMessage: ZLB / with-AVP round-trip, header flag byte
      constant, version, length-match, plus 8 malformed-input rejects.
    * seq_delta / seq_advance: zero, positive/negative no-wrap,
      positive/negative across wrap, half-window boundary, wrap on advance.
    * Peer.send: Ns assignment + advance, remote CCID stamping,
      current Nr piggyback, empty-AVPs rejection, retransmit queueing.
    * Peer.receive: in-order deliver, duplicate re-ack, out-of-order
      within window (reorder queue drains when hole fills), outside
      window drop, ZLB no-op, wrong-CCID drop.
    * Ack consumption: piggybacked Nr and peer-ZLB both flush queue.
    * Retransmit: RTO trigger, exponential back-off progression, cap
      at 16s, dead-peer after max_retries, dead sticks, Nr refresh.
    * LoopbackTransport: two-peer HELLO exchange + ack; lossy-drop
      case where first datagram is dropped and retransmit recovers.
- **pytest-asyncio** added as an optional test dep (auto-mode in
  `pyproject.toml` so async tests need no decorator).

## 0.1.0

- **First release of XCESP's L2TPv3 (RFC 3931) dynamic-mode
  control-plane daemon.**  This is the first free-software Linux
  implementation of the L2TPv3 control channel — OpenL2TP is
  L2TPv2-only, go-l2tp's ql2tpd is static+keepalive between two
  ql2tpd instances (not RFC 3931 negotiation), and prol2tp is
  proprietary.
- **Scope of 0.1.0** — subproject skeleton + AVP codec + unit
  tests only.  No wire I/O.  Sets up the module layout the
  0.2.x-0.9.x releases will fill in per the roadmap plan file.
- **AVP codec** (`xcesp_l2tpv3d/avp.py`) — encode/decode of the RFC
  3931 §5.2 AVP header (M/H bits, reserved, 10-bit length, vendor
  ID, attribute type) plus per-type value serialisation for the
  mandatory AVP set required by SCCRQ/SCCRP/SCCCN/ICRQ/ICRP/ICCN
  message building in future releases: Message Type (0), Result
  Code (1), Random Vector (36), Message Digest (59), Router ID
  (60), Assigned Control Connection ID (61), Pseudowire
  Capabilities List (62), Local Session ID (63), Remote Session
  ID (64), Assigned Cookie (65), Remote End ID (66), Pseudowire
  Type (68), L2-Specific Sublayer (69), Data Sequencing (70),
  Circuit Status (71), Preferred Language (72), Tx Connect Speed
  (74), Rx Connect Speed (75).
- **Hidden-AVP path** (RFC 3931 §4.3) — MD5-keystream encryption
  of Attribute Value using a shared secret + Random Vector AVP;
  round-trips cleanly with a shared-secret fixture.
- **Unit tests** (`test/test_avp.py`) — round-trip every
  implemented AVP type, boundary lengths (empty value, 1-byte,
  256-byte, max 1017-byte), malformed input rejection (short
  header, over-long length, unknown vendor+type).  Runs via
  `make test`.
